lesson k-dots-at-a-glance v=1 format=teach grade=0 unit=1 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Dots at a glance"
  goal "See how many dots there are, up to six, without counting them."

  look {
    scene 11x6 {
      dice d faces=[3, 4, 5] at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
        On a dice the dots make shapes: three is a line, four is a square, and five is a square with
        one in the middle. Once you know the shape you know the number, without counting.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked k.dice-glance n=3 level=medium
    }
    practice k.dice-glance count=3 seed=2 easy-count=2
    practice k.frame-glance count=2 seed=3
    practice k.two-dice count=2 seed=4 easy-count=1
    practice k.same-number-dots count=1 seed=5
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show k.quick-or-count
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show k.make-six-dots a=2 t=6 easy-a=4 hard-a=3 hard-t=7
  }
  remember {
    say "The shape of the dots tells you the number."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Read each question aloud and show the picture only briefly if you can, so the child reads the
    shape rather than counting. Counting to five taught touching each thing once; this lesson is the
    step on from it, knowing small numbers at a glance. If the child counts every dot, play a board
    game with a dice and say the number together before moving. Off the page, flash fingers and ask
    how many. England, Reception, ELG Number: "Subitise (recognise quantities without counting) up to
    5".
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level three dots are read in the teacher's pen first, the dice show one to three, and the two dice make five or less."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the dice show four to six, the two dice make seven or eight, and the three-star total is seven."
  }
}
